Subject: phonics and kanji

Phil asks, if look-say is so lame, how can Japan have such a tremendous
literacy rate?  Phonics can't help with kanji.

Partial answer:  Phonics can too help with kanji, some.  Most kanji have a
"sounds like" element (giving the Chinese sound, of course) and a classifier
element.  Thus, each of five (?) kanji that sound like "ma" (`horse') have
HORSE as an element.

Partial answer:  They don't.  All Japanese know the kana (about a hundred
phonetic signs), but far fewer can read a newspaper, from what I hear.

Partial answer:  Look-say would have limited application to all-kana text,
because (I think) they don't write word breaks.

