Subject: LAW: Employment contracts

I would like to apologize to any list member who feels this discussion has
been a waste of bandwidth. I would like to express my gratitude to Tom for
engaging in a discussion that I (at least) have enjoyed.

>From: twb3@midway.uchicago.edu (Tom Morrow)
>[Tom]
>>>[Tom]
>>[Dale Burns]

That's Dave Burns.

>[. . .] employees can still smell bad in a society that allows for free
>contracting about such matters.  They just have to find a willing employer.

And they can still have sex, read books, believe in god or not. They just
have to find a willing employer.

> So there is no loss in the possible range of behaviors (though there may
>be a price to pay) by allowing free contracting about such matters. [. . .]

Similarly, in the 'right to smell society' there is no loss in the possible
range of behaviors (though there may be a price to pay). All that changes
is what is customary and what is exceptional. 

Here Tom has helped me to find a difficulty in my argument. I'm assuming
that both societies have a common law system which means (to me) that any
law can be contracted around and any contract can be challenged in court.
There is an apparent contradiction here. What good is a contract that can
be challenged legally, and what good is a precedent or ruling that can be
contracted around? Everything seems arbitrary and up in the air. The logic
of it sucks, but in practice common law systems seem to be able to
function. Custom, habit, and tradition perhaps play a role in smoothing
things out. The contradiction serves as an adjustment mechanism.

Here is a scenario that Tom should use against me. The employer and
employee write a contract forbidding the employee to smell bad. The
employee later sues to get out of smelling good without losing his job. The
judge rules in favor of the employee, setting a precedent that smelling
good should not be part of the terms of an employment contract. The
employer still wants to avoid employees that smell bad, and there ought to
be a way to contract around the ruling, since no ruling is presumed to be
perfect. So the employer changes the contract but is still just as
vulnerable to the whims of the court. What is missing is some process by
which both parties can verifiably demonstrate informed consent, precluding
any later dispute over terms. 

This must be the crux of disagreement. (?) What establishes informed
consent? I am embarrassed to admit that I've never understood a single
contract I've ever signed. The only thing that makes me willing to sign
them is the knowledge that a) lots of other people are signing identical
contracts, some of whom are or have lawyers and b) I know that the courts
have sympathy for idiots like me c) bad contracts are bad business.

Where does this leave me? My position has changed and (I hope deepened). I
don't think that it is obvious that any clause that an employer wishes to
put in a contract and an employee is willing to sign should be enforced.
I'm not clear on how informed consent should be established. But I'm
waffling on the issue of whether or not there ought to be some process by
which parties should be able to make and enforce a highly controversial
agreement.
 
