Dale said a number of things that parallel peripheral concerns of mine...
> . . . .  *There might be thoughts you are
> unable to think.*  (Or maybe you can think them only in the most
> abstract, symbolic way, and not in an emotional, motivating way.)

As my .sig says.  I have wanted for a while to write a longish piece here
about creole logic (to borrow Mark Miller's language), but haven't found
much that I can say.  ;)  So I'll just repeat a small point:

We know of many optical illusions -- ways in which our seeing-apparatus
tells us things that ain't so.  In some cases they have obvious adaptive
origins, like the size/distance illusion.  Mark (in Extropy) showed that
there is a good reason for the animators' bouncing ball illusion.  Might
there be analogous logical illusions?  I think it not unlikely that some
mathematical proofs rest on flaws that we simply cannot see -- after all,
the only assurance we have of a proof's correctness is that mathematicians
have looked at it and not seen flaws.

> . . . . I
> know that I have sensory evidence that my mental functions are not
> unitary, but I still perceive my consciousness to be unitary:
> [describes incident]

I've had a similar experience recently.  While under the influence of hemp
smoke, I can't consciously detect some of my faculties -- my time-sense goes
funny, and I can't tell whether I am speaking coherently for more than a
(brief) sentence at a time.  But (on the occasion I have in mind) several
people did listen to me at length, and tell me later that they enjoyed it.
So some part of what I would normally call my conscious mind retained an
ability that another part could not detect.  "Awareness" itself may be a
small and unimportant function (that should start some flames).

(I have since decided to stop smoking socially, as I can't remember what I
might have said that was so interesting.)

Similarly, while drugged I sometimes seem to have poor balance, but as far
as I can tell (from admittedly suspect memory) I'm no clumsier than usual.

To change the subject from drugs, I've noticed that I consistently flinch
from a loud noise "before" hearing it.  That is, the sensation of the flinch
reaches my awareness before that of the sound.

> So I propose that the "unity of consciousness" is a hardwired idea of
> the human brain, and that the possibility of our genes waging war on
> our memes is not pure speculation...

