I won't second Dave's apology to list members disinterested in our
discussion, since I think this is an entirely appropriate subject for this
forum.  But I will bounce back his gracious "thank you" and apologize for
mistakenly calling him Dale.

>. . .in the 'right to smell society' there is no loss in the possible
>range of behaviors (though there may be a price to pay). 

I have to question this, since the "right to smell" society forbids
employees from enforcing contracts that pay them a premium for cleanliness.
 

>I'm assuming
>that both societies have a common law system which means (to me) that any
>law can be contracted around and any contract can be challenged in court.

This strikes me as a counterfactual assumption.  Common law does *not*
allow you to contract around any law.  Just to use an example that I am
quite familiar with, common law will not let you enforce a contract to
assign another person the right to litigate your personal injury tort
claims.  Nor does this assumption seem true to our discussion up to this
point.  I, at least, was assuming that the society that forbids cleanliness
employment contracts does just that; it doesn't pretend that such contracts
are forbidden and then allow people to contract around the law!

>There is an apparent contradiction here. What good is a contract that can
>be challenged legally, and what good is a precedent or ruling that can be
>contracted around? Everything seems arbitrary and up in the air. 

Exactly.  That's why commonlaw doesn't work as you describe it.

>This must be the crux of disagreement. (?) What establishes informed
>consent? 

The Uniform Commercial Code has widely accepted criteria for establishing
informed consent, which have been derived from common law traditions.  I'm
pretty satisfied that they are about as good a set of rules as humans can
generate.

I had asked for Dave to frame the argument against allowing cleanliness
employment contracts in property-language.  He offered:

>Privacy involves an ownership claim of information. 

But this works in exactly the opposite direction.  If employees really had
property rights in their personal information, then they could transfer
those rights to their employers.  Forbidding such transfers puts the
property right in  the regulator's hands--but then it cannot be sensibly
called "personal information" because it is no longer a matter of personal
discretion.

>If you refuse
>authorship, I still need a handle by which to refer to the argument,
>perhaps the "employers' argument."

I don't refuse authorship, so saying "Tom says" is entirely appropriate. 
But you should not presume that you know what I believe, or what my "stand"
is.  I ask only that you distinguish between what I *say* when we're
playing on this intellectual jungle-gym and what I *really believe.*  

Trying to figure out the latter trait is futile, anyhow, since I try to
avoid saying I "believe" anything.  The word is too near "faith."  I prefer
to say that I have "working hypotheses," though the distinction is
admittedly a fine one.

