From: D Anton Sherwood <dasher@well.sf.ca.us>
Subject: Schelling point bias

In a gedanken-experiment on Schelling points, I got a funny answer.

Here is how I've heard Schelling points illustrated.  You are told to meet
someone in St Louis, but you're a stranger to the city and to the other
person.  So you go to the Gateway Arch, because the Arch is one thing
everyone knows.

I was explaining the concept to my father Bruce, who happens to have a
number of points in common with David Friedman: 
	a doctorate in physics;
	a connection (past or present) with the University of Chicago;
	a fondness for science fiction.  

Substitute Chicago for St Louis, and let Bruce and David be the subjects.
Now, just knowing that each is acquainted with me, and knowing my interests,
should give them some ideas about Schelling points.  (Knowing that the other
is taking part in a Schelling experiment is itself a big clue, as Douglas
Hofstadter might or might not say.)

Suppose I tell each that the other is familiar with Chicago, but without
mentioning the University; instead I tell David that Bruce is a physicist,
and Bruce that David is a science-fiction fan.  Then it's more likely that
Bruce will go to the planetarium, while David goes to Fermilab!

Thus the Schelling property depends on equal ignorance.

The problem rarely arises in this form, but Schelling points seem important
to any spontaneous order.  I think David said a few months ago that the
common law can be seen as a compilation of S-points in rule-space; the value
of the common law is less that it meets a cosmic standard of justice, than
that everyone knows what it is.  S-points, and/or Bayes analysis, also help
us understand elliptical language.

Have I overestimated the importance of equal ignorance?  What techniques do
we use to prevent or compensate for inequalities?

