  Re: logical illusions in mathematical proofs

   I don't think it is possible for the mind to be blind to logical flaws
because the logic we use is _defined_ by our minds. That is, mathematicians
can start out by saying ``these are our axioms and operations of logic and
they are true because we have defined them. Now we can construct lots of
neat theorems by applying our defined logic.'' An example might be
quantum logic (distributive property fails). Normally, it is counterintuitive,
but mathematicians can define a quantum logic system and then use it to
prove proofs. To a normal person, a quantum logical proof would look flawed
but to a quantum mathematician it is perfectly acceptable. If the
mind simulates a logic system, all proofs generated by such a system will
be consistent within that system. (unless the system itself is
internally inconsistent which you would have to show. I don't know
Godel says this is decidable or not).

   In a sense, the mind does simulate logic just like neural
nets and genetic algorithms simulate XOR, but such logic is no less real
than computer logic. The mind also simulates conciousness, emotions, and
life, but what is the point of stating such a thing? Subjectivist agenda?
   Unless you have some proof that our minds simulate an inconsistent
or incomplete logic, I don't buy your claim until you can show some
evidence. (remember Ken Tolman's claim that he had a disproof of the
Church-Turing thesis based on `xeno' machines? He got roasted in
comp.theory and sci.math because he couldn't come up with a single
calculation that his xeno machines could compute which a turing machine
couldn't.)

  I hope this thread doesn't degenerate into another subjectivism vs
objectivism thread. 




