From: hal@alumni.cco.caltech.edu (Hal Finney)
Subject: EVOL: Extropian Challenge (was New forms overtaking old  ...)

From: Ed_Lane@novell.com (Ed Lane)

> In a small isolated town called "Dogpatch" a woman takes a man in
> marriage and assumes his last name.  All their [immediate] progeny also
> assume this same last name.
> 
> Question:  Will everyone eventually have the same last name?

I'd say probably so, since you have no mechanism for creating new
names, but you do have a mechanism for eliminating old ones (the last
male holder of a name has no male children).  However, you also have a
mechanism for everyone in Dogpatch to die, and no mechanism for
starting the town again.  My guess is that the names would die out
before the town.

> In *the world* assume that the same marriage conventions of "Scenario
> 1" exist.
> 
> Question 1:  Will everyone eventually have the same last name?

Yes again, although here your assumptions are quite artificial, because
it is unlikely that no new name-forming mechanism would occur over
a long period.

> Bonus 1: If Q1 then *when* will it happen?
I would not expect heavy correlation between names and progeny over
large periods, so I'd see it as a random walk, with slow random changes
in the number of people with each name.  Once each name randomly walks
into the case of 0 people, it never walks away (random walk with one
absorbing barrier).  Given specific assumptions on the number of people
and percentage of names, along with mean and variance on number of children,
you could work out the numbers from standard random walk equations.

> Bonus 2: If Q1 then *what* will that name be?
No way to tell in my scenario, although those names which are widely used
today (Chinese names mostly) would be likelier than less common ones.

> How is the result of Scenarios 1 and 2 affected by:
> 	1) polygamy (Utah?)
> 	2) divorce/adoption (US-at-large?)
Either of these might help with the case of a person who knew he was
the last of his name, and who wanted to keep the name alive - these would
give more options for the case where for some reasons he couldn't have
male children with his wife.  This would reduce the rate of name extinction,
but probably not eliminate it entirely.

> 	3) female fetalcide (India?)
If all females were killed, the race would go extinct before the names did,
reversing my answer to the questions above.

> What are the implications of "Scenario 2" to memetics?
> Do old memes [last names] ever die?
I see little connection.  Memes can leap generations by being written
down.  Look at modern libertarians drawing their influence from writers
in the 19th and early 20th centuries, ideas which were once thought
defeated.  A meme can't die in the sense that a name can in your (artifical)
scenarios.

